  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 6.07 — Where Next\n\nForty-three notebooks ago this journey started with dot products and lines in $\\mathbb{R}^3$. This one is a map of the territory that begins where it stops, written honestly: what each direction is, what it needs, and what it would cost to enter.\n\n## The thread that ran through everything\n\nOne object kept reappearing, and it is worth naming before looking outward.\n\n| notebook | it appeared as |\n|---|---|\n| 1.01 | the cross product $a \\times b$ |\n| 1.03 | rotations of space, and unit quaternions |\n| 5.01 | $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$, with $[\\hat v,\\hat w] = \\widehat{v\\times w}$ |\n| 5.03 | the curvature of $SU(2)$: $K = \\tfrac14|[X,Y]|^2$ |\n| 5.04 | one bracket sign separating $S^2$ from $H^2$ |\n| 6.03 | $\\{L_i,L_j\\} = \\varepsilon_{ijk}L_k$, on phase space |\n\n**The cross product of first-year vector algebra and the curvature of the 3-sphere are the same fact.** That is the shape of the whole subject: a small number of structures, met repeatedly in different clothes.\n\nKlein's question from 2.01 — *what group, and what does it preserve?* — answered every stage. Euclidean geometry is $E(n)$; projective is $PGL$; Riemannian geometry replaces the global group with a local one and calls the failure to integrate it **curvature**; gauge theory (5.05) lets the group vary from point to point on purpose.\n\n## Four directions out\n\n### Index theory\n\n$$\\text{analytic index} = \\text{topological index}$$\n\n5.06 turned curvature into integers. The Atiyah–Singer index theorem does the same for **differential operators**: the dimension of the solution space of an elliptic operator, minus the dimension of its cokernel, equals a characteristic-class integral. Gauss–Bonnet, Riemann–Roch and the Hirzebruch signature theorem are all special cases.\n\n*Needs:* elliptic operator theory, K-theory, spin geometry. *Entry point:* Roe's *Elliptic Operators, Topology and Asymptotic Methods*.\n\n### Ricci flow and geometrisation\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial g_{ij}}{\\partial t} = -2R_{ij}$$\n\nA heat equation for the metric. Hamilton introduced it in 1982; Perelman used it in 2002–3 to prove the Poincaré conjecture and Thurston's geometrisation conjecture. The flow smooths curvature but develops singularities, and the technical achievement is controlling them by surgery.\n\n4.05 computed $\\mathrm{Ric}$; this is what happens if you let it drive the metric.\n\n*Needs:* geometric analysis, parabolic PDE, comparison geometry (4.06). *Entry point:* Topping's *Lectures on the Ricci Flow*.\n\n### Geometric analysis\n\nThe Yamabe problem, minimal surfaces (3.09) in their modern form, harmonic maps, the positive mass theorem. The common shape: a geometric question becomes a nonlinear elliptic PDE, and the analysis decides the geometry.\n\n*Needs:* real analysis, Sobolev spaces, elliptic regularity. *Entry point:* Jost's *Riemannian Geometry and Geometric Analysis*.\n\n### Metric geometry\n\nGromov's programme: keep distance, discard smoothness. Gromov–Hausdorff convergence turns the set of *all* metric spaces into a metric space; CAT($\\kappa$) spaces generalise curvature bounds to spaces with no tangent vectors; geometric group theory studies groups by making them into metric spaces.\n\n4.06's comparison theorems survive this transition — which is the point of them.\n\n*Needs:* metric topology, some geometric group theory. *Entry point:* Burago, Burago and Ivanov's *A Course in Metric Geometry*.\n\n## Smaller steps, if the above are too large\n\n- **Lie theory properly**: root systems, weights, the classification of simple Lie algebras. Stage 5 used $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ and $\\mathfrak{sl}(2,\\mathbb R)$ and stopped. Fulton and Harris.\n- **Algebraic geometry**: Stage 2's projective geometry over $\\mathbb{C}$ with sheaves. Hartshorne's *Algebraic Geometry* — a different book from the one this journey cited.\n- **Numerical relativity**: 6.02's metrics, evolved. 6.06's mesh machinery is the beginning of it.\n- **Optimal transport**: a metric on the space of probability measures, sitting beside 6.05's Fisher metric and behaving very differently. Villani.\n\n## What this journey did not cover\n\nStated plainly, so the gaps are visible:\n\n- **Spinors and Clifford algebras**, beyond 6.01's $SL(2,\\mathbb C)$ remark. Half of index theory lives here.\n- **Sheaves and cohomology** as tools rather than as words. 5.06 used de Rham cohomology and never defined it.\n- **Foliations, contact geometry** — the odd-dimensional sibling of 6.03.\n- **Analysis, throughout.** Every convergence and existence question was waved past. That is the single largest gap, and it is exactly what the geometric-analysis direction repairs.\n- **Proofs.** This journey computed and verified. It did not prove Birkhoff's theorem (6.02), Newlander–Nirenberg (6.04), Chentsov's theorem (6.05) or the no-free-lunch theorem (6.06); each was used and flagged.\n\n## A closing note on method\n\nEvery notebook here executed its own claims. That discipline caught a Riemann index lowered into the wrong slot (4.04), a `ctensor` cache returning the previous metric's answer (4.04), a scaling factor of $y^2$ against $y^2/2$ (4.03), a bisection bracketing a non-monotone function (3.09), a Lorentzian signature making two sectional curvatures agree that the prose said differed (4.05), and a Gauss–Bonnet integrand divided by $W$ instead of $W^{3/2}$ (5.06).\n\nNone of those were found by re-reading. All were found because a number that should have been zero was not, or because $\\chi$ came out proportional to a radius.\n\n**That is the argument for computing rather than only reading.** Not that the algebra is hard, but that a written derivation can be wrong in a way that looks entirely plausible, and an executed one usually cannot.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\neps(i,j,k) := (j-i)*(k-i)*(k-j)/2$\n\n/* ---------------------------------------------------------------\n   The running thread, computed four ways in one place.\n   --------------------------------------------------------------- */\nbr(X,Y) := ratsimp(X.Y - Y.X)$\n\n/* (1) the cross product of basis vectors (1.01) */\ncrs(a,b) := [ a[2]*b[3]-a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1]-a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2]-a[2]*b[1] ]$\nee : [[1,0,0],[0,1,0],[0,0,1]]$\n\n/* (2) so(3) as matrices (1.03, 5.01) */\nLb : [ matrix([0,0,0],[0,0,-1],[0,1,0]),\n       matrix([0,0,1],[0,0,0],[-1,0,0]),\n       matrix([0,-1,0],[1,0,0],[0,0,0]) ]$\nvec3(M) := [ M[3][2], M[1][3], M[2][1] ]$\n\n/* (3) su(2) as 2×2 complex matrices (5.01) */\nEb : [ matrix([0,-%i/2],[-%i/2,0]),\n       matrix([0,-1/2],[1/2,0]),\n       matrix([-%i/2,0],[0,%i/2]) ]$\ncoordE(M) := [ ratsimp(2*%i*M[1][2]), ratsimp(2*M[2][1]), ratsimp(2*%i*M[1][1]) ]$\n\n/* (4) angular momentum as functions on phase space (6.03) */\nqs : [q1,q2,q3]$  ps : [p1,p2,p3]$\npb(f,g) := ratsimp( sum( diff(f,qs[i])*diff(g,ps[i])\n                       - diff(f,ps[i])*diff(g,qs[i]), i, 1, 3) )$\nLf : [ q2*p3-q3*p2, q3*p1-q1*p3, q1*p2-q2*p1 ]$\n\nprint(\"ONE ALGEBRA, FOUR INCARNATIONS.  For each pair (i,j), the\")$\nprint(\"difference between the bracket and ε_ijk (basis element k):\")$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   i j    cross product   so(3) matrices   su(2)   Poisson bracket\")$\nfor i:1 thru 3 do for j:i+1 thru 3 do block([k, c1, c2, c3, c4],\n  k : 6 - i - j,\n  c1 : crs(ee[i], ee[j]) - eps(i,j,k)*ee[k],\n  c2 : vec3( br(Lb[i],Lb[j]) - eps(i,j,k)*Lb[k] ),\n  c3 : coordE( ratsimp(br(Eb[i],Eb[j]) - eps(i,j,k)*Eb[k]) ),\n  c4 : ratsimp( pb(Lf[i],Lf[j]) - eps(i,j,k)*Lf[k] ),\n  print(\"   \", i, \" \", j, \"     \", c1, \"     \", c2, \"     \", c3,\n        \"        \", c4) )$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Zero in every column.  1.01's cross product, 1.03's rotations,\")$\nprint(\"5.01's Lie algebra and 6.03's Poisson brackets are one object.\")$\nprint(\"5.03 added that the same bracket IS the curvature of SU(2),\")$\nprint(\"K = ¼|[X,Y]|², and 5.04 that flipping one of its signs turns the\")$\nprint(\"sphere into the hyperbolic plane.\")$\n\n/* ---------------------------------------------------------------\n   A first step into one of the directions above: Ricci flow.\n   --------------------------------------------------------------- */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"RICCI FLOW ∂g/∂t = −2 Ric, on the round sphere.\")$\nts(e) := ratsimp(trigsimp(e))$\nchristoffel_hand(g, vars) := block([ginv, n_],\n  n_ : length(vars),  ginv : ratsimp(invert(g)),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( sum( ginv[k][l]*( diff(g[j][l], vars[i]) + diff(g[i][l], vars[j])\n                        - diff(g[i][j], vars[l]) ), l, 1, n_)/2 ),\n    j,1,n_), i,1,n_), k,1,n_) )$\nriem_hand(g, vars) := block([G, n_],\n  G : christoffel_hand(g, vars), n_ : length(vars),\n  makelist( makelist( makelist( makelist(\n    ts( diff(G[l][j][k], vars[i]) - diff(G[l][i][k], vars[j])\n        + sum( G[l][i][m]*G[m][j][k] - G[l][j][m]*G[m][i][k], m, 1, n_) ),\n    k,1,n_), j,1,n_), i,1,n_), l,1,n_) )$\nricci_hand(g, vars) := block([Rr, n_],\n  Rr : riem_hand(g, vars), n_ : length(vars),\n  genmatrix( lambda([i,j], ts( sum( Rr[k][k][i][j], k, 1, n_) )), n_, n_) )$\n\nassume(Rr0 > 0, u > 0, u < %pi)$\nghat : matrix([1,0],[0,sin(u)^2])$          /* the UNIT round metric */\ngR   : Rr0^2 * ghat$\nRicR : ricci_hand(gR, [u,v])$\nprint(\"   g = R²ĝ on S²  ⟹  Ric = \", RicR, \" = ĝ, independent of R\")$\nprint(\"   so −2Ric = −2ĝ, and ∂g/∂t = (dR²/dt) ĝ gives\")$\nprint(\"      dR²/dt = −2(n−1) = \", -2*(2-1), \"   for n = 2\")$\ndepends(A, tv)$\nsolR : ode2( 'diff(A, tv) = -2, A, tv )$\nprint(\"   ode2 for R²(t): \", solR)$\nremove(A, dependency)$\nprint(\"   R²(t) = R₀² − 2t, so the sphere shrinks to a point at\")$\nprint(\"      T = R₀²/2 :\", \"  R₀ = 1 → T = \", 1/2,\n      \"   R₀ = 3 → T = \", 9/2)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"      t      R²(t)   (R₀ = 3)\")$\nfor tc in [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4.5] do\n  print(\"     \", tc, \"      \", float(9 - 2*tc))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"   The metric reaches zero in FINITE time.  That is the simplest\")$\nprint(\"   Ricci-flow singularity, and controlling the general case — by\")$\nprint(\"   surgery, through the singular times — is what Perelman's proof\")$\nprint(\"   of the Poincaré conjecture required.  4.05 computed Ric; this is\")$\nprint(\"   what happens when it is allowed to drive the metric.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
