   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## The journey, in one table\n\n| stage | question | answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | What does distance preserve? | $E(n)$, and its finite subgroups |\n| 2 | What if the group changes? | projective, inversive, spherical, hyperbolic |\n| 3 | How is curvature measured from outside? | second fundamental form; $K = \\kappa_1\\kappa_2$ |\n| 4 | How is it measured from inside? | $R^l_{\\;ijk}$, the failure of $\\nabla$ to commute |\n| 5 | What if the group is the space? | Lie groups, bundles, curvature as field strength |\n| 6 | Where is any of this used? | relativity, mechanics, statistics, meshes |\n\nThree results held the stages together, each proved in one and paid off in another:\n\n- **Theorema Egregium** (3.05): curvature is intrinsic. Without it Stage 4 has no subject.\n- **Gauss–Bonnet** (3.08): $\\int K\\,dA = 2\\pi\\chi$. Reappeared as the Euler class (5.06), as the Fubini–Study area (6.04), and as an exact identity on a four-vertex mesh (6.06).\n- **Killing–Hopf** (4.07): three simply connected constant-curvature models, which are exactly Stage 2's three geometries.\n\n## Verified, not asserted\n\nThe final cell puts the running thread in one place: for every pair $(i,j)$, the bracket minus $\\varepsilon_{ijk}$ times the $k$-th basis element came out **zero in all four realisations** — cross product, $\\mathfrak{so}(3)$ matrices, $\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ matrices, Poisson brackets of angular momentum.\n\nAnd one step into Ricci flow, since it is the direction nearest to what Stage 4 built: for $g = R^2\\hat g$ on $S^2$, $\\mathrm{Ric} = \\hat g$ **independent of $R$**, so $\\partial g/\\partial t = -2\\mathrm{Ric}$ becomes $dR^2/dt = -2$ and\n\n$$R^2(t) = R_0^2 - 2t.$$\n\nThe sphere shrinks to a point in finite time $T = R_0^2/2$ — the simplest Ricci-flow singularity there is. Controlling singularities in general, by surgery, is what Perelman's proof required.\n\n## References\n\n- Roe, J. (1998) *Elliptic Operators, Topology and Asymptotic Methods*. 2nd edn. Boca Raton: Chapman & Hall/CRC. The shortest honest route to Atiyah–Singer.\n- Topping, P. (2006) *Lectures on the Ricci Flow*. London Mathematical Society Lecture Note Series 325. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press. Available free from the author; assumes Stage 4 and little else.\n- Jost, J. (2017) *Riemannian Geometry and Geometric Analysis*. 7th edn. Cham: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-3-319-61860-9](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-61860-9). Covers Stage 4 again, then continues into the analysis this journey omitted.\n- Burago, D., Burago, Y. and Ivanov, S. (2001) *A Course in Metric Geometry*. Graduate Studies in Mathematics 33. Providence: American Mathematical Society. Curvature without smoothness.\n- Gromov, M. (1999) *Metric Structures for Riemannian and Non-Riemannian Spaces*. Boston: Birkhäuser. The programme itself, in Gromov's own voice — difficult, and worth attempting once the above is in hand.\n- Villani, C. (2009) *Optimal Transport: Old and New*. Grundlehren der mathematischen Wissenschaften 338. Berlin: Springer. The other geometry on spaces of measures, beside 6.05's.\n\n---\n*None of this notebook's references were consulted directly; all are given from knowledge. The two code computations above were executed. Everything else here is a signpost, not a result — the point of the section is to be honest about where the journey stops.*",
   "metadata": {}
